    content: "There's a question I get almost every week:\n\n\"I'm selling, but it's not investment real estate. Is a 1031 my only option?\"\n\nNo. And that's the part most sellers never hear.\n\nA 1031 exchange is powerful, but it only works for like-kind investment or business-use real estate. It does nothing for the assets where large gains often hide:\n\nA primary residence in a market that quadrupled.\nA business you spent 30 years building.\nA concentrated stock position.\nA crypto holding you bought early.\n\nFor those, there's a different tool: the 537 Installment Sale Trust, built on IRC Section 453.\n\nInstead of paying the full capital gains bill the year you sell, you structure the sale as an installment note to an irrevocable trust. You defer the tax and pay it as you receive payments, spread across a term that is often 10 to 30 years.\n\nSame goal as a 1031, different mechanism, wider range of eligible assets.\n\nThe sellers who navigate this well don't pick the tool first. They start with the asset and the timeline, then match the structure to it.\n\nSelling something a 1031 won't cover? Send me a message and I'll walk you through whether a 537 IST may fit.\n\nEducational only, not tax, legal, or investment advice. Consult your own advisors. Provided by Q-1031.",
