    content: "He called me on day 46.\n\nHe'd sold a fourplex, fully intending to do a 1031. Then the replacement deal collapsed in escrow, the 45-day identification window slammed shut, and suddenly he was staring at a capital gains bill he never planned for.\n\nHis first words: \"I think I'm out of options.\"\n\nHe wasn't necessarily.\n\nWhen a 1031 falls apart after the sale, there's still a path many people don't know exists. The 537 Installment Sale Trust works on a different section of the code, IRC 453, and it isn't bound to the same 45/180-day deadlines.\n\nIn the right situation, a seller who blew the 1031 window may still be able to structure the proceeds to defer the gain instead of writing one giant check in April.\n\nIt's not automatic, and it's not for everyone. Timing, how the sale was documented, and the asset all matter. But \"the exchange failed\" and \"there's nothing left to do\" are not the same sentence.\n\nIf a 1031 just went sideways on you, don't assume the door is closed. Call me at (719) 714-1565 before you accept the tax bill as final.\n\nEducational only, not tax, legal, or investment advice. Consult your own advisors. Provided by Q-1031.",
