    content: "Quick myth-buster for a Friday.\n\nMyth: \"Like-kind\" means I have to swap my rental house for another rental house.\n\nReality: \"Like-kind\" is one of the most misunderstood phrases in real estate.\n\nFor 1031 purposes, like-kind refers to the nature of the property, not its specific type. Investment and business-use real estate is generally like-kind to other investment and business-use real estate.\n\nThat means a single-family rental can potentially exchange into:\n\nAn apartment building.\nRaw land.\nA retail strip center.\nIndustrial or warehouse space.\n\nWhat it does NOT include: your primary residence, a property you're flipping as inventory, or your personal vacation home.\n\nThe \"same type\" myth quietly talks a lot of tired landlords out of exploring an exchange that may have worked just fine. They assume they're stuck, sell, and pay the gain they could have deferred.\n\nDon't let a misunderstood word make a six-figure decision for you.\n\nWhat's a 1031 myth you believed before you learned otherwise? Drop it below, I'll clear up the most common ones in the comments.\n\nEducational only, not tax, legal, or investment advice. Consult your own advisors. Provided by Q-1031.",
