    content: "There's a phrase I've watched describe thousands of real estate investors: land rich, cash poor.\n\nIt's the landlord sitting on millions in appreciated property who still feels strapped, because every dollar of equity is locked inside buildings that demand tenants, repairs, and 2 a.m. phone calls.\n\nI cared about that problem enough to write a book about it. \"Land Rich, Cash Poor\" came out of 20-plus years specializing in installment sales and capital-gains deferral, and watching the same trap close on good people over and over.\n\nHere's the trap in one sentence: the wealth is real, but selling to access it triggers a tax bill big enough that many landlords just don't, and stay tired instead.\n\nThe whole point of tools like a 1031 exchange or a 537 Installment Sale Trust is to break that false choice. You shouldn't have to pick between keeping a portfolio you've outgrown and handing a large share of your gain to the IRS the moment you sell.\n\nThere's often a structured path between those two extremes. Most people just never get shown it.\n\nIf \"land rich, cash poor\" describes someone you know, or you, that's exactly the conversation we exist to have.\n\nChristian Ramsey, AIF®, author and tax-deferral and installment-sale specialist.\n\nEducational only, not tax, legal, or investment advice. Consult your own advisors. Provided by Q-1031.",
