    content: "Here's a scenario that trips up even experienced investors.\n\nYou find the perfect replacement property. It's available now. But you haven't sold your current property yet, and in a competitive market you can't afford to wait until you do.\n\nMost people assume that kills the 1031. Buy first, lose the deferral, right?\n\nNot necessarily. That's exactly what a reverse 1031 exchange is for.\n\nIn a reverse exchange, you acquire the replacement property BEFORE selling the relinquished one. An Exchange Accommodation Titleholder temporarily holds title to one of the properties so the transaction still satisfies IRC 1031.\n\nIt's more complex than a standard forward exchange and the structuring has to be precise. But for an investor who can't risk losing the right property to a slow sale, it can be the difference between landing the deal and watching it go to someone else.\n\nThe catch: this has to be set up correctly and in advance. You can't reverse-engineer it after you've already closed on the new property.\n\nIf you're eyeing a replacement before your current property sells, that's the moment to talk, not after.\n\nFound a property you can't let slip? Comment or send a message and I'll explain how the reverse structure works.\n\nEducational only, not tax, legal, or investment advice. Consult your own advisors. Provided by Q-1031.",
