    type: "Native Post",
    theme: 'light',
    author: { name: 'Q-1031 Exchange Solutions', handle: 'q-1031', avatar: '/avatars/q-1031.svg', verified: false, headline: 'Capital Gains Tax Deferral · 1031 Exchange & 537 Installment Sale Trust · Nationwide', connectionDegree: '1st' },
    content: "Selling a business creates a tax problem most owners don't see coming until the offer is on the table.\n\nTake a FedEx route owner, or really any small business seller, who's built something worth a couple million dollars over a decade or more.\n\nA buyer shows up. Great news. Then the seller does the math on the capital gains and realizes a big slice of the sale price is about to disappear in a single tax year.\n\nA 1031 won't help here. It's for real estate, not a business sale.\n\nThis is where the 537 Installment Sale Trust earns its keep.\n\nInstead of taking the full sale proceeds and the full tax hit at once, the owner sells to an irrevocable trust and receives payments over time. The capital gains tax is deferred and paid as those installments arrive, not all up front.\n\nThere's a second benefit that's easy to overlook. A tax-efficient exit can make the seller more willing to offer favorable terms to the buyer, which may help the whole deal close more smoothly.\n\nThe best time to set this up is BEFORE you sign, ideally while you're still negotiating. Once the deal closes, the easy structuring options narrow.\n\nThinking about selling a business or a route in the next year or two? Let's talk while you still have room to structure it. q-1031.com.\n\nEducational only, not tax, legal, or investment advice. Consult your own advisors. Provided by Q-1031.",
