    title: "Three mistakes that quietly wreck a 1031",
    pillar: "F",
    pillarLabel: "Engagement",
    scheduledISO: "2026-06-26T08:00:00",
    hashtags: "#1031Exchange #RealEstateInvesting #TaxStrategy #CapitalGainsDeferral #InvestingMistakes",
    visualDescription: "Numbered checklist graphic '3 Mistakes That Wreck a 1031' with three rows, each paired with a red warning icon: a hand grabbing cash, a torn-off calendar page, and an ineligible house with an X. Brand-colored header.",
    type: "Native Post",
    theme: 'light',
    author: { name: 'Q-1031 Exchange Solutions', handle: 'q-1031', avatar: '/avatars/q-1031.svg', verified: false, headline: 'Capital Gains Tax Deferral · 1031 Exchange & 537 Installment Sale Trust · Nationwide', connectionDegree: '1st' },
    content: "I've seen exchanges fall apart for the same handful of reasons, over and over. None of them were complicated. All of them were avoidable.\n\nHere are three that catch good investors off guard.\n\n1. Touching the money.\nThe moment sale proceeds hit your personal account, the exchange can be over. The funds have to flow through a Qualified Intermediary from the start. This is the most common, most painful, and most preventable mistake of them all.\n\n2. Waiting too long to identify.\nThe 45-day identification clock is unforgiving. Investors who start property-hunting AFTER closing are already behind. The serious ones are scouting replacements before they ever list.\n\n3. Assuming an asset qualifies when it doesn't.\nPrimary residences, flips held as inventory, and personal-use property generally don't qualify. Finding that out at closing is a brutal surprise. Better to confirm eligibility early, while you still have alternatives like a 537 IST on the table.\n\nNotice the common thread: every one of these is a timing problem disguised as a rules problem. Start early enough, and most of them simply never happen.\n\nWhich of these surprises you most? And if you've watched a fourth mistake play out, add it below so others can learn from it.\n\nEducational only, not tax, legal, or investment advice. Consult your own advisors. Provided by Q-1031.",
