    content: "Picture a couple who bought a home in California decades ago.\n\nThey paid a few hundred thousand. Today it's worth several million. On paper, they won the lottery.\n\nThen they decide to sell and downsize, and discover the catch nobody warned them about.\n\nAfter the standard primary-residence exclusion, a large share of that appreciation is still exposed to capital gains tax. For a long-held home in a market that climbed sharply, that can mean a tax bill big enough to change retirement plans.\n\nAnd here's the part that stings: a 1031 exchange can't help. That tool is for investment property, not the home you live in.\n\nThis is one of the clearest cases to look at a 537 Installment Sale Trust.\n\nRather than absorbing the entire capital gains hit in the year of the sale, the homeowners may be able to structure the sale so the tax is deferred and paid as they receive payments over time, often across many years. The proceeds can keep working in the meantime instead of being reduced up front.\n\nIt has to be set up correctly and before the sale closes. But for a primary residence carrying decades of gain, it's a strategy far too few homeowners ever hear about.\n\nSitting on a long-held home with a gain that worries you? Let's look at it together before you list. Call (719) 714-1565.\n\nEducational only, not tax, legal, or investment advice. Consult your own advisors. Provided by Q-1031.",
