    title: "Myth: A 1031 Has to Be a Same-for-Same Swap",
    pillar: "F",
    pillarLabel: "Engagement",
    scheduledISO: "2026-06-27T11:00:00",
    hashtags: "#1031Exchange #LikeKindExchange #1031Myths #CapitalGainsDeferral #RealEstateInvesting #RealEstateMyths #InvestmentProperty #TaxStrategy #1031ExchangeTips #CommercialRealEstate #PropertyInvestor #NationwideServices",
    visualDescription: "Single image, bold myth-vs-truth split. Top band 'MYTH' in muted gray with two identical duplex icons and an equals sign. Bottom band 'TRUTH' in brand purple showing varied property icons (land, warehouse, apartment, retail) all connecting to one exchange symbol. Center divider text: 'Like-kind ≠ same-for-same.' Small disclaimer along bottom 15%.",
    type: "Single Image",
    theme: 'light',
    author: { name: 'Q-1031 Exchange Solutions', handle: 'q1031exchange', avatar: '/avatars/q-1031.svg', verified: false },
    content: "MYTH: \"Like-kind\" means I have to trade my duplex for another duplex. ❌\n\nTRUTH: \"Like-kind\" is far broader than people think. For real estate, many investment or business-use properties can generally be exchanged for many others.\n\nRaw land for an apartment building. A retail strip for a warehouse. A rental house for a share of a larger commercial property. As long as both sides are real property held for investment or business use, they generally qualify.\n\nWhat \"like-kind\" does NOT mean: trading real estate for crypto, stock, or your primary home — those need a different tool entirely (that's where the 537 IST comes in).\n\nKnowing the real rule can open up options many sellers never consider.\n\nEducational only, not tax, legal, or investment advice — confirm with your own advisors.\n\nThought it had to be identical? Share this with an investor who needs to see it. ♻️",
